# Skips CI is allowed to see, and the reason each one is not coverage
# being quietly dropped.
#
# `.github/scripts/skip_census.sh` fails a job on any skip at all. That is
# the right default: a feature-detected suite that declines to run still
# exits zero, and four milestones were accepted while partial because
# nothing said so. Every remaining skip must name a missing environmental
# prerequisite; a platform implementation gap is not entitled to a
# declaration here.
#
# Format, one declared skip per line:
#
#   declared|<platform>|<marker>|<why this skip is not a dropped test>
#
#   <platform>  `uname -s` lowercased (linux, darwin), or `any`.
#   <marker>    a substring of the skip line the suite prints. Matching
#               on the reason rather than the test name keeps one line
#               covering every suite that shares the reason.
#
# Rules the checker enforces, mirroring .github/enforcement-expectations:
#
#   - a skip whose line matches no declared marker fails the job;
#   - a declared marker for *this* platform that matched no skip also
#     fails the job, because the skip has stopped being necessary and
#     this file has started lying;
#   - declarations for other platforms are inert here, so the same file
#     is read the same way by every job.
#
# Deleting a declaration is correct only when its skip has become unnecessary.

# --- macOS: the MCP teardown assertion has no process-observation API.
#
# The fixture requires `/proc/<pid>` observation before reserving its runtime.
# Darwin has no procfs, so this prerequisite skips the whole fixture before
# the MCP exchange or shutdown runs. This declaration stops matching when the
# fixture gains a Darwin process observation and the census demands removal.
declared|darwin|no /proc on this host, so a stopped server's death cannot be observed by pid|Darwin has no procfs, so the prerequisite skips the whole real-MCP fixture before setup

# The first-party language profiles on macOS (`conformance/lsp_profiles_test`,
# ADR-014 §6). Two of the three cannot run as shipped on a hosted macOS
# runner, for want of something the host lacks. Go comes from the runner's
# tool cache under /Users, outside the Seatbelt system view, and the
# lsp_go profile, like any table, cannot grant a GOROOT it does not know;
# its README says to copy the table into loom.toml with GOROOT added. The
# runner has no rust-analyzer component. lsp_gleam runs on both macOS lanes
# without a waiver, and the jailed Linux lane runs all three for real.
declared|darwin|SKIP lsp profile lsp_go: go's GOROOT|The runner's Go lives in its tool cache under /Users, outside the jail's system view the shipped profile relies on; the jailed Linux lane runs lsp_go's checks for real.
declared|darwin|SKIP lsp profile lsp_rust: rust-analyzer|macOS runners carry no rust-analyzer component; the jailed Linux lane installs it and runs lsp_rust's checks for real.

# The language-server manager's live rust-analyzer fixture
# (`client/lsp/manager_test`) on the advisory macOS check. macOS runners
# carry no rust-analyzer component, and rustup's link without it does not
# run, so the fixture's prerequisite check skips it. The Linux client
# bucket installs the component, rust-src and std's dependencies
# (.github/scripts/install_rust_analyzer.sh) and runs it for real.
declared|darwin|SKIP lsp manager rust-analyzer: rust-analyzer does not run|macOS runners carry no rust-analyzer component; the Linux client bucket installs it and runs the fixture for real.
